title: an eplanation
pid: b965b0e4-2bdc-4f53-8752-1bce809e8198
body: |2-
  To present is to, as writing, contain.

  Books present chapters, which present subchapters ...

  An atom can be a presenter in some relationships (it presents other atoms)
  and can be the presented in some relationships (other atoms present it).

  Most writing throughout history has been hierarchical,
  but two atoms can present the same atom.
  The same story, for instance, might illustrate multiple principles,
  so (the atom representing) each principle
  might present (the atom representing) that story.

  When this happens, [[id:a0144ae1-a281-4c9f-9694-364188c4c369][the forest contains overlapping branches]].
  More specifically, the forest defined by the 'presents' relationship.
  When it does not happen, the writing is hierarchical:
  each atom is presented by only one presenter.
